#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028 :topic/difficulty 0.8139321757155085 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428422 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account linear: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: linear=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/linear-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1867->3029. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "26e7d665-1fe7-5eaf-8ec8-486cb95ff36f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119661 :question/id #uuid "7cce27e9-e2b2-599c-9f4e-437caf1a23ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-5415" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $4 \\times 7$ matrix that has $3$ **non-pivot** columns. Find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$. \n\nSince $A$ has $7$ columns and $3$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n7-3=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be3d5a74-259e-58b9-948d-47ba7412ab08" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Rank of a Matrix Given the Number of Pivot Columns"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119662 :question/id #uuid "a6a3db41-6033-537a-bc9d-03fde02dc869" :question/math-academy-id "e-5417" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4?$\n\n\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\quad C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a matrix of rank $4$ must have at least $4$ columns and at least $4$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $B$ since it has only $3$ rows. \n\nAlso, the matrix must have $4$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $A$ has only $3$ pivot columns, while the matrix $C$ has exactly $4$ pivot columns. So, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3$ and $\\textrm{rank}(C)=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d847ec9-c379-5013-ab77-a9b92dc8ca27" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix With a Given Rank by Inspection"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119663 :question/id #uuid "b3e4eb01-0669-586b-a716-aef87d1c3580" :question/math-academy-id "e-5418" :question/problem "If \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n, find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the rank of $A,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 35\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $3$ pivot columns (or, alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5a44c9-16e5-55fb-a10f-15435a0a9a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank of a Matrix Using Row Reduction"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1867 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :topic/title "The Rank of a Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996 :topic/difficulty 0.5951061045948267 :topic/id #uuid "7821b684-8118-5d42-9459-ebb20010e0ff" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124508 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124317 :question/id #uuid "e1036a11-ebb3-594d-aba1-be259eb0596d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5419" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $9 \\times 11$ matrix that has $9$ pivot columns, then what is $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))?$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))$ is the number of non-pivot columns in $A$. \n\nSince $A$ has $11$ columns and $9$ of them are pivots, then the number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n11-9=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=2$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce5ddb87-f7c0-5bda-8d6e-ac1728dd2f99" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix Given the Number of Non-Pivot Columns"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124509 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124318 :question/id #uuid "4aa47a89-9f9b-55d8-ba01-a131b20e6c95" :question/math-academy-id "e-5421" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If ![](images/77/77c0dd8d9b925b3310c5470f272b59e2b9d25bb141295741f1748771b6c454c1.svg) then $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=2$\n\nII. If ![](images/86/86c4f440750b5d7bf45e8c36b6fd9852c2749ac56331b3e0d76a6901acaf5b60.svg) then $\\textrm{dim(Null}(B))=0$\n\nIII. If ![](images/22/2295be7f70c3de36b51784b2919af95695f5520abdb2300287a17852b4cbfce3.svg) then $\\textrm{dim(Null}(C))=1$" :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The matrix $A$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $2$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The matrix $B$ has $1$ pivot column and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $2$nd and $3$rd columns). So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(B))=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The matrix $C$ has $2$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$nd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f020b27f-1cd4-550b-a83a-2afbb7e5db72" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Dimension of the Null Space of Matrices Using Inspection"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124510 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124319 :question/id #uuid "42d77ab8-0670-55bf-8b99-68270c8e5f2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5422" :question/problem "Given ![](images/82/829e66e040815801f77e92b2afd97531779864935faf7c9dcf1b0485593db856.svg) what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A))$, we reduce $A$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$rd column). \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{nullity}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Null}(A)) = 1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3a0e3b9-931a-52bd-9ed9-b8001c8de67b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Nullity of a Matrix Using Row Reduction"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1868 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049903}] :topic/title "The Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix"}]}
